# The installation this machine becomes. Read by install-machine.sh and install-machine.ps1 at a
# machine's birth, and by regenerate-install-branch.sh and regenerate-install-branch.ps1 whenever an
# installation is brought to the release its own map is pinned to.
#
# key=value and not JSON: a shell reads it with one `.` and needs no parser, and a
# comment is the only place a value can say what it is for.
#
# Single quotes, nothing is expanded. A value carrying a single quote is refused
# before the machine is touched. A list is
# comma-separated; the catalogue says which values are lists.

# SECRETS. Each carries `secret: true` in the program that declares it. Eight are
# credentials a third party issued; the ninth raises commands to root.

ELEVATION_PASSWORD=''
PLATFORM_REPO_WRITE_PAT=''
PLATFORM_REPO_READ_PAT=''
# A Cloudflare token for DNS ONLY: Zone:DNS:Edit and Zone:Zone:Read, scoped to this
# installation's own zone and with no account-level permission. A token for another
# Cloudflare product fits this line and cannot read a zone, and nothing notices until the
# first unit's record (hostyour-deploy#28).
CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN=''
# A SECOND Cloudflare token, for object storage, and a MANAGING one: Workers R2 Storage
# Write on the account named below. The platform makes each tenant's bucket with it and
# mints a key reaching that one bucket, so no tenant ever holds this token. Not the zone
# token above: neither can do the other's work, and each fits the other's line.
CLOUDFLARE_R2_API_TOKEN=''
STORAGE_BOX_PASSWORD=''
REGISTRY_DOCKERHUB_TOKEN=''
BUILD_PLATFORM_REPO_WRITE_PAT=''
BUILD_MANAGER_REPO_WRITE_PAT=''
BUILD_CATALOG_REPO_WRITE_PAT=''
# read-only on CATALOG_REPO: what a machine's reconciler reads this installation's tenant
# charts with. The deployment programs need none — their repository is public.
CATALOG_REPO_READ_PAT=''


# SETTINGS. What the five programs declare. Each is refused by name if missing.


OPERATOR_USER=''
PLATFORM_REPO=''
# the branch deploy-host CLONES the platform tree on — 'master' for a machine born as a master
PLATFORM_BRANCH=''
# THE STATE THIS MACHINE IS BORN ON, as a release tag such as 0.7.9-stable-20260903202414.
# deploy-branch merges it into the branch it cuts and writes it as the `release:` pin of this
# cluster's map, which is what every later regeneration reads. It is NOT PLATFORM_BRANCH above:
# that one names what is cloned, this one names the state the install branch is put on. A tag has
# to stand on the platform repository already, and a FIRST machine has no install branch for one to
# be cut against: release-platform.sh with a version and a channel and NO fqdn mints the tag, pushes
# it and pins nothing, which is the form that answers this key.
PLATFORM_REF=''
OPERATOR_PUBLIC_KEY=''
FQDN=''
LOGIN_SERVER=''
# one of [dev, test, prod]
STAGE=''
# every part this machine carries — one of [master, slave, master+slave]
ROLE=''
# THE CLUSTER THAT KEEPS THE BOOKS — the cluster maps and the registrations this installation
# knows about itself. Empty where this cluster keeps them itself; the other cluster's domain
# where it does not, which is what a slave states.
BOOKS_FQDN=''
BUILD_PLANE_FQDN=''
UNIT_APEX=''
PLATFORM_DOMAIN=''
# a list: separate entries with a comma
ALERT_RECIPIENTS=''
# owner/name of THIS INSTALLATION'S OWN repository of tenant charts — private, reached with
# CATALOG_REPO_READ_PAT. Not the repository the programs come from; that one is DEPLOY_REPO.
CATALOG_REPO=''
# owner/name of the public repository the deployment programs are read from. The platform's
# own, the same for every installation, and cloned without a credential.
DEPLOY_REPO='simetrixch/hostyour-deploy'
LAN_HOST=''
COMMITTER_NAME=''
COMMITTER_EMAIL=''
LETSENCRYPT_EMAIL=''
STORAGE_BOX_HOST=''
STORAGE_BOX_USER=''
# may stay as the declared default platform-pull
REGISTRY_PULL_USER=''
# may stay as the declared default platform-push
REGISTRY_PUSH_USER=''
LETSENCRYPT_SERVER=''
# THE TIME SERVERS THIS INSTALLATION'S MACHINES ASK, comma-separated on one line, written into
# chrony's sources by deploy-host, which then refuses while the clock stays out of step. REQUIRED:
# deploy-host empties the pools the distribution names, so a machine with no answer here would ask
# no time server at all, and the program refuses before its first step. Name the provider's where
# the provider answers no other (Hetzner answers ntp1.hetzner.de, ntp2.hetzner.de, ntp3.hetzner.de
# and drops the rest).
TIME_SOURCES=''
# THE UNIT THAT IS THIS INSTALLATION'S MAIL SERVICE, by its unit name, written into the map as
# endpoints.mail.unit by deploy-branch. For this product that unit is the consumer digita-post. It
# is stated before that unit is onboarded, because the address is the unit's and not the cluster's:
# every unit composes https://<unit>-<its own stage>.<unit apex> itself, so a test unit reaches the
# test mail service and a prod unit the prod one, and the map names no address at all. A tenant's
# auth and report members require it and stop their render without it, so leave it empty ONLY on an
# installation that runs no mail service: an empty value is left out of the map, and the members
# then say what they lack by name.
MAIL_UNIT=''
# THE ACCOUNT THE TENANT BUCKETS ARE MADE IN, as the 32-character id the Cloudflare dashboard
# shows, and the jurisdiction they are made under — `default`, `eu` or `fedramp`. Neither is a
# secret: the account stands in every bucket's public endpoint and travels in the cluster map,
# where the tenant charts read it to compose one. Answer both together with the token above or
# none of the three: a tenant creation on an installation missing any of them is refused by
# name, which is where it has to be noticed.
#
# THE JURISDICTION IS PART OF A BUCKET'S IDENTITY, not a setting on it. Changed after the first
# tenant exists it does not move that tenant's bucket; it hides it.
CLOUDFLARE_R2_ACCOUNT_ID=''
CLOUDFLARE_R2_JURISDICTION='default'
# WHICH AUTHORITY ISSUES THIS INSTALLATION'S CERTIFICATES: platform-acme is the public one, whose
# root every browser and container image already carries; platform-local is one the cluster mints
# for itself, which nothing outside the installation trusts. An installation begins on
# platform-local and is flipped to platform-acme once its names resolve.
#
# STATE IT EVEN WHEN IT IS platform-local. An empty value here is not "no answer" the way it is
# above: the programs default it to platform-local, so leaving it empty on an installation that
# stands on platform-acme makes the next regeneration rewrite its issuer and re-issue every
# certificate from the cluster's own root - which the public certificates it already holds were
# rate-limited to obtain.
CLUSTER_ISSUER='platform-local'
VERSION=''
# one of [alpha, beta, stable]
CHANNEL=''
# may stay as the declared default http://127.0.0.1:8484
MANAGER_URL=''
